A||N||3||chester||Charging issues.||||8||
Z||000000||chester||08-29-2001||03:21 PM||||Hi all,<P>I was having some charging issues in the past where the alternator charge would drop off after running the car for a few.  I assumed that it was the alternator so I replaced it with a high amp unit.  It seemed to be fine for a couple of days.  I went to the Lebanon NH show and on the way there everything was great.  Voltage stayed around 13 volts and everything seemed fine.  Untill the return trip that is.  On the trip home the voltage guage started spazing out. It would show overcharge then drop and not charge and be erratic as hell all the way home.  Now it does it all the time.  When I first fire it up the voltage sits where it should.  About a minute into it running the guage does the wiggy dance all over the place.  it eventually drops out to nothing and the battery dies.  I have a BRAND NEW battery and alternator.  The plug to the alternator was iffy so I replaced it with no help.  I do have the heat sheild on the alternator.  Any suggestions?  I have done a search on the forum and have come across simular posts but there were no solutions listed.  HELP!!!!<P>Rob D.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigline.jpg">   <BR>Modified 2.8<BR>2.5" Drop<BR>11" Brakes<BR>17" Revolutions<BR>RCC Coilover Suspension||||reg||8
Z||000001||starfighter007@msn.com||08-29-2001||03:40 PM||||you you could a bad gauge and it is shorting out when you hit a bump or a wire that runs from gauge to battery or still have a bad alternator its not unheard of to have a bad rebuild alt. go to auto ze and have them verify alt trnator is still good they can check it while its still on the car||||reg||1
Z||000002||chester||08-30-2001||08:58 AM||||**BUMP**||||reg||1
Z||000003||bad911||08-30-2001||10:21 AM||||Sounds like your prob is runtime/heat cycle and could be a loose/corroded terminal/connection. Go thru your charging and gauge circuits and verify clean, tight connections, be critical about good grounds at the engine and frame. If you don't find anything, your alt might be heat sensitive. If you go have it tested it likely will not be detected faulty unless it is in its failure mode. Chasing intermittent alternators is not fun. What engine do you have? Are you running the stock gauge? Be careful, charging systems can self-destruct and even bite. Good luck.  <p>[This message has been edited by bad911 (edited 08-30-2001).]||||reg||1
